Draw a quad's four boundary edges under polygon-mode lines, not the fan spokes.

Under glPolygonMode(..., GL_LINE), gl4es drew a different edge set for the
same quad depending only on how many quads shared a glBegin/glEnd:

  - two or more quads per block go through renderlist_quads2triangles() to
    GL_TRIANGLES, and fill_lineIndices() recovers the original topology from
    mode_init == GL_QUADS, emitting the four boundary edges.  Correct.

  - a lone quad is relabelled GL_TRIANGLE_FAN by end_renderlist() - a
    4-vertex fan *is* the quad, so no indices are expanded and the conversion
    is free - but `case GL_TRIANGLE_FAN` in fill_lineIndices() only
    special-cases mode_init GL_QUAD_STRIP and GL_POLYGON.  With no GL_QUADS
    arm it fell through to the generic fan reconstruction, which emits the
    spokes:

        (0,1) (0,2) (1,2) (0,3) (2,3)

    Five edges, the extra (0,2) being the diagonal that splits the quad.

The `len==4` sub-branch under `case GL_TRIANGLES` / `case GL_QUADS`, which
looks like it handles the single-quad case, is unreachable for the same
reason: a 4-vertex quad list never has mode == GL_TRIANGLES.

The ring that the GL_POLYGON arm already walks over [z, len) is exactly the
four boundary edges of a 4-vertex block, so GL_QUADS simply joins that arm.

Native GL draws four edges for a quad however it is batched.  Measured on
Mesa 4.6 (Compatibility Profile) / llvmpipe through surfaceless EGL, on the
same geometry the browser oracle uses:

    one quad          367 px   (perimeter 368, +diagonal would be 504)
    4 in one glBegin  1468 px  (perimeter 1472)
    4 in four blocks  1468 px

gl4es before this patch: 485 / 1468 / 1939 - the batched case already
matched, the other two did not.  After: 1119 / 1468 / 1468 against a 1120 px
perimeter for the oracle's larger lone quad, and 1468 for both four-quad
spellings, which is native to the pixel.

GL_QUAD_STRIP had the same shape of bug, and is fixed here too.
end_renderlist() relabels it to GL_TRIANGLE_STRIP - the vertex order is
already a valid triangle strip, so nothing is reordered - and `case
GL_TRIANGLE_STRIP` in fill_lineIndices() had no mode_init check at all, so a
quad strip came out with the strip's diagonal across every quad.  Quad q is
bounded by v(2q) -> v(2q+1) -> v(2q+3) -> v(2q+2), so those four edges are
emitted instead; adjacent quads share a rung and emit it twice, coincident,
which is also what native does when it draws each polygon's boundary.

Measured on a 4-quad ladder, 640x480, against a 1624 px calibrated outline
(rails plus rungs):

    native (Mesa/llvmpipe)   1620 px
    gl4es before             2124 px   (one diagonal per quad)
    gl4es after              1620 px

Note the pre-existing `mode_init==GL_QUAD_STRIP` arms under GL_TRIANGLE_FAN
and GL_TRIANGLES are NOT reused, because they are wrong: for a 10-vertex strip
they emit the rungs plus *both* diagonals of each quad and no rails at all.
They are unreachable, which is why that was never visible; this patch leaves
them alone rather than widening its scope.

Guarded by test_quad_has_no_diagonal() and test_quad_strip_boundary_edges()
in packaging/web/bench/gl4es_edge_flag.c, which check the lone quad, both
four-quad spellings, and the quad strip (with and without a cleared edge
flag) against their calibrated outlines.

